:complexity-complex
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Forests characteristically showing all or most of the following properties:
•	The tallest stratum, excluding emergent, has many species.
•	A large range of structural features, e.g. plant buttresses, spur buttresses, unbuttressed stems, compound leaves, simple leaves, lobed and deeply divided leaves, strap like leaves. 
•	A large range of growth forms, none of which tends to dominate, e.g. trunk bases usually obscured by climbing pandans, palms, ferns and aroids; robust and slender lianes are present; complex understorey consisting of shrubs, seedlings of larger trees, palms, gingers, pandans and ferns.
•	The vegetation is not usually arranged into distinguishable, discrete strata.
•	The stem diameters of the tallest non-emergent trees are usually uneven in size."""@en ;
    skos:notation "C" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Complex"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:complexity-simple
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Forests showing most or all of the following properties:
•	The tendency for one or a few species to dominate the canopy, e.g. coachwood or Antarctic beech.
•	A reduced number of structural features, e.g. plant buttresses absent, or most stems unbuttressed or with star buttresses.
•	A tendency for one or two growth forms to be more conspicuous, e.g. trunks not usually obscured by climbing plants and epiphytes, but when they do one growth form usually dominates; or understorey layers may have a conspicuous growth form such as a tree fern layer, ground fern layer, shrub or palm layer.
•	The stem diameters of the canopy trees are usually uniform in size.
•	Discrete strata, e.g. a tree fern layer, an understorey tree layer or shrubs."""@en ;
    skos:notation "S" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simple"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:complexity-simple-complex
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Forests with features of simple and complex forests. Use this category if in doubt or if the vegetation does not possess at least four out of the five properties listed for the other categories."@en ;
    skos:notation "X" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simple-complex"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:cover-abundance
    a
        skos:Collection ,
        sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "Braun-Blanquet cover–abundance scale" ;
    skos:definition """Cover–abundance combines cover and abundance to estimate the quantity of each species in a vegetation sample. For cover values greater than 5%, the scale is a measure of cover (see above). For cover values less than 5%, it measures abundance, that is, the number of individuals in a defined area.

The Braun-Blanquet cover–abundance scale (Table 19) is the most widely used. It is simple to use, but produces estimates of cover–abundance that are robust for most vegetation classification processes. The system is based on the fact that vegetation is often highly variable and so it is more useful to have many samples to show this variation than to have few precise and time-consuming measures.

Record the cover class code values for the class that represents each species at the sample site.

For the less dense cover classes, imagine moving all individuals into one area and compare that with a reference for the sample site. For example, if the sample site is 400 m2, 5% of the area is 20 m2 (4 m × 5 m), and 1% is 4 m2 (2 m × 2 m).

Although this method provides an absolute value for classes 2–5 (that is, it is a percentage of a defined sample area), the class boundaries are wide and cover is estimated, not measured. Many studies have shown large variations between observers, as well as in one observer’s estimates at different times. Because of these inherent errors, it is important to regularly compare observers if more than one person is making records and to check observations if one person is recording.

In broad-ranging surveys, it is usually better to have large numbers of samples with good cover–abundance estimates, rather than a few precise measures that do not cover the variability of the vegetation. If the objectives of the survey are narrowly focussed and looking for fine levels of discrimination between samples or sampling times, then actual quantitative measurements are more appropriate than class values."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:member
        :cover-abundance-1 ,
        :cover-abundance-2 ,
        :cover-abundance-3 ,
        :cover-abundance-4 ,
        :cover-abundance-5 ,
        :cover-abundance-cross ,
        :cover-abundance-r ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cover abundance"@en ;
.

:cover-abundance-1
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Many individuals that cover <1/20 of the sample site, or scattered with cover up to 1/20 of the sample site"@en ;
    skos:notation "1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "<5%"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:cover-abundance-2
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Any number of plants covering from 1/20 to ¼ of the sample site"@en ;
    skos:notation "2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "5-25%"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:cover-abundance-3
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Any number of plants covering ¼ to ½ of the sample site"@en ;
    skos:notation "3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "25-50%"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:cover-abundance-4
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Any number of plants covering from ½ to ¾ of the sample site"@en ;
    skos:notation "4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "50-75%"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:cover-abundance-5
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Any number of plants covering more than ¾ of the sample site"@en ;
    skos:notation "5" ;
    skos:prefLabel ">75%"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
    schema:citation "Source: modified from Mueller-Dombois and Ellenberg (1974)." ;
.

:cover-abundance-cross
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "(pronounced ‘cross’) Few individuals with small cover"@en ;
    skos:notation "+" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Insignificant cover"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:cover-abundance-r
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Single individual with small cover"@en ;
    skos:notation "r" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Insignificant cover"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:crown-cover-class
    a
        skos:Collection ,
        sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "The recorded cover values collected using the methods below can be converted to cover classes as shown in Table 17. Crown cover classes are those used in Walker and Hopkins (1990) and these were selected to coincide as closely as possible with the cover classes of previous classifications, e.g. the projective foliage cover classes of Specht et al. (1974)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:member
        :cover-class-D ,
        :cover-class-E ,
        :cover-class-I ,
        :cover-class-L ,
        :cover-class-M ,
        :cover-class-S ,
        :cover-class-V ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crown cover class"@en ;
    skos:related
        :crown-cover ,
        :crown-separation-ratio ,
        :foliage-cover ;
.

:emergent-non-sclerophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Common non sclerophyllous rainforest emergents include Agathis, Podocarpus, Araucaria, Flindersia and Erythrina."@en ;
    skos:notation "A" ;
    skos:prefLabel "non-sclerophyllous rainforest emergents "@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:emergent-sclerophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Common sclerophyllous emergents over rainforest include species of Eucalyptus, Corymbia, Acacia, Syncarpia, Casuarina, Lophostemon and Melaleuca."@en ;
    skos:notation "E" ;
    skos:prefLabel "sclerophyllous emergents "@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:emergent-taxon
    a
        skos:Concept ,
        sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Emergent taxon"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Emergent taxon"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:emergents
    a skos:Collection ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Some plants can rise above the level of the dominant stratum and because their total cover is small, they are considered to be emergents rather than a separate stratum. As a guideline, emergents are recognised if their foliage cover is less than 5% of the crown cover of the dominant stratum (see Figure 8 for a tree example). Care is needed in classifying tall plants as emergents, and the 5% guideline can be expected to vary depending on vegetation type and season – especially if the ground layer is the dominant stratum. In some borderline cases, taller plants can occur over a lower stratum that elsewhere forms the dominant stratum of a well-known vegetation type. In this situation it is acceptable to continue to call the tallest stratum emergents where it is considered unhelpful to create a new vegetation type solely on the basis of the unusually slightly higher cover of the tallest plants.

Record the total crown cover percentage, median height and/or maximum height, and the genus (and species if possible) of the emergent layer. Measure height and cover as for other plant attributes described earlier.

When the tallest stratum is not the most significant stratum, the guidelines vary for different kinds of vegetation as follows. 
•	Where the vegetation is dominated by trees or shrubs, and the tallest layer emerges above a dominant canopy (that is, cover >5%) and has generally less than 5% total cover, then the tallest trees or shrubs are called emergents. The genus or species of emergents should be recorded, if possible, followed by the word ‘emergents’: for example ‘with hoop pine emergents’; ‘with Araucaria emergents’; ‘with Eucalyptus emergents’. 
•	Where the vegetation is dominated by perennial grasses, for example Triodia, and a taller layer of woody plants emerges above it with less than 5% of the Triodia cover, then the tallest plants are called emergents and should be named as in the example above.
•	Where the vegetation is seasonally or sporadically dominated by annual plants in a mix of perennial plants that form a taller layer, then in most cases the dominant layer is the taller perennial layer. For example, ‘sparse eucalypt trees with seasonally dominant Sorghum in the understorey’ or ‘sparse acacia trees with periodically dominant annual herbs of Asteraceae and other families’. 
•	For ephemeral wetlands, where the dominant layer is only present periodically and there is no taller woody layer, the dominant layer is the ephemeral layer. It is recorded as ephemeral, for example: ‘ephemeral mixed herbs’."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:member
        :crown-cover ,
        :emergent-taxon ,
        :maximum-height ,
        :median-height ;
    skos:prefLabel "Emergents"@en ;
.

:ground-cover-class
    a
        skos:Collection ,
        sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "The ground layer normally comprises low shrubs, grasses, forbs, rushes, sedges etc., and it is necessary, in this classification, to estimate the foliage cover as a vertical projection. For many purposes a visual estimate will suffice to place the ground cover into a cover class (Table 17). "@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:member
        :cover-class-D ,
        :cover-class-E ,
        :cover-class-I ,
        :cover-class-L ,
        :cover-class-M ,
        :cover-class-S ,
        :cover-class-V ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ground cover class"@en ;
    skos:related
        :crown-cover ,
        :crown-separation-ratio ,
        :foliage-cover ;
.

:growth-form-a20
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "genera and species of flowering angiosperms of the families Hydrocharitaceae and Potamogetonaceae, forming sparse to dense mats of material at the sub-tidal level and down to 30 m below mean sea level. Occasionally exposed."@en ;
    skos:notation "a2.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seagrass: marine"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-a30
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "a member of the Chlorophyta, Cyanophyta, Phaeophyta or Rhodophyta living in fresh or brackish aquatic environments."@en ;
    skos:notation "a3.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Algae: fresh or brackish"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-a40
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "a member of the Chlorophyta, Cyanophyta, Phaeophyta or Rhodophyta living in marine environments. May range from thin surface-hugging layers to tall algal forests."@en ;
    skos:notation "a4.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Algae: marine"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-b10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "soil, rock or water surfaces with less than 0.5% plant cover."@en ;
    skos:notation "b1.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bare surface"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-c10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "assemblages of one or more species of minute plants at or within the surface of soil or rock. May consist of bryophytes, lichens, cyanobacteria, green algae and fungi; may in some cases include very small vascular plants."@en ;
    skos:notation "c1.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Surface crusts"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-cryptogam
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "cryptogam refers collectively to lichens and bryophytes."@en ;
    skos:narrower
        :growth-form-l10 ,
        :growth-form-m10 ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cryptogam"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-f10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "a member of the Division Pterophyta, i.e., ferns and fern allies. Characterised by large and usually branched leaves (fronds); herbaceous and terrestrial to aquatic; spores in sporangia on the undersides of leaves. Tree ferns are classified with woody plants as they have the same vegetation structure."@en ;
    skos:notation "f1.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fern (excluding tree ferns)"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-food
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "see Shrub: planted/cultivated (food) or Tree: planted/cultivated (food)."@en ;
    skos:narrower
        :growth-form-w13 ,
        :growth-form-w34 ;
    skos:prefLabel "Food"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-g10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "member of the family Poaceae."@en ;
    skos:notation "g1.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Grass"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-g20
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "coarse xeromorphic grass with a mound-like form often dead in the middle; genera are Triodia, Plectrachne and Zygochloa."@en ;
    skos:notation "g2.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hummock grass"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-g30
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "grasses forming discrete but open tufts usually with distinct individual shoots. These include the common agricultural grasses."@en ;
    skos:notation "g3.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tussock grass"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-g40
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "member of the Poaceae planted or cultivated for specific human uses, e.g. human or other animal food, lawn or other ground cover."@en ;
    skos:narrower
        :growth-form-g41 ,
        :growth-form-g42 ,
        :growth-form-g43 ;
    skos:notation "g4.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Grass: planted/cultivated"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-g50
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "herbaceous, usually perennial, erect plant generally with a tufted habit and of the families Cyperaceae (true sedges) or Restionaceae (node sedges)."@en ;
    skos:notation "g5.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sedge"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-g60
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "herbaceous, usually perennial, erect monocot that is neither a grass nor a sedge. For the purpose of this chapter, rushes include the monocotyledon families Juncaceae, Typhaceae, Liliaceae, Iridaceae, Xyridaceae and the genus Lomandra, i.e., ‘graminoid’ or grass-like genera."@en ;
    skos:notation "g6.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rush"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-h10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "non-graminoid herbaceous plant."@en ;
    skos:notation "h1.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forb"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-h20
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "herbaceous or slightly woody, annual or sometimes perennial plant (dicotyledon or monocotyledon)."@en ;
    skos:narrower
        :growth-form-h21 ,
        :growth-form-h22 ,
        :growth-form-h23 ,
        :growth-form-h24 ;
    skos:notation "h2.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Herb"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-v10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "climbing, twining, winding or sprawling plants usually with a woody stem."@en ;
    skos:notation "v1.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Vine"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-w10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "woody plant more than 2 m tall usually with a single stem, or branches well above the base; not always distinguishable from large shrubs."@en ;
    skos:narrower
        :growth-form-w11 ,
        :growth-form-w12 ,
        :growth-form-w13 ,
        :growth-form-w14 ;
    skos:notation "w1.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tree"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-w20
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "plants with woody tissues. For the purposes of vegetation classification here, also those plants that achieve a growth form similar to that of woody plants (e.g. cycads, palms, tree ferns). Includes both trees and shrubs."@en ;
    skos:notation "w2.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Woody plant (indeterminate tree or shrub)"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-w21
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "any of the eucalypt trees or shrubs with multiple stems arising from a lignotuber."@en ;
    skos:notation "w2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mallee (tree or shrub)"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growth-form-w30
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "woody plant, multi-stemmed at the base (or within about 200 mm from ground level), or, if single-stemmed, less than about 5 m tall; not always readily distinguishable from small trees."@en ;
    skos:narrower
        :growth-form-w31 ,
        :growth-form-w32 ,
        :growth-form-w33 ,
        :growth-form-w34 ,
        :growth-form-w35 ;
    skos:notation "w3.0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shrub"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growthstage-advanced
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Trees dominant	- Dominated by dense to sparse, well-developed, immature plants. Large emergents can be present with crown cover <5% of the total crown cover. However, if the cover is >5%, classify as ‘uneven age’. Trees have well-developed stems (poles). Crowns have small branches. The height is below maximum height for the stand type. Apical dominance still apparent in vigorous trees.

Shrubs dominant	- Dominated by dense to sparse, well-developed but not mature plants. If large emergent plants are present, then they occupy <5% crown cover of the dominant stratum; if >5%, classify as ‘uneven age’.

Grasses and herbs dominant	- Vegetative growth abundant; plants approaching full mature size but reproductive material absent or in early stages only; soil surface largely obscured in average sites.

Cryptogams dominant (mosses and lichens) - Cover of plants high for the site; some reproduction may be evident."""@en ;
    skos:notation "2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Advanced regeneration"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growthstage-early
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Trees dominant	- Dominated by small, juvenile, dense to very sparse regenerating plants, with or without a few older, widely spaced, emergent plants.	

Shrubs dominant	- Dominated by small, juvenile, dense to very sparse regenerating plants. A few older, widely spaced emergents may be present.	

Grasses and herbs dominant	- Plants small and juvenile stages predominate; bare soil or old litter common.	

Cryptogams dominant (mosses and lichens) - Thin growth of young plants or widely spaced clumps of young plants."""@en ;
    skos:notation "1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Early regeneration"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growthstage-mature
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Trees dominant	- Well-spaced mature-sized plants or densely packed plants with crowns touching, with or without emergent senescent plants. Trees at maximum height for the type and conditions. Crown at full lateral development in un-locked stands. No apical dominance.

Shrubs dominant	- May have well-spaced mature-sized plants, or have very densely packed plants with crowns touching, with or without emergent senescent plants.

Grasses and herbs dominant	- Most plants of reproductive age; depending on vegetation type, reproduction evident, or would be if environmental conditions were appropriate (e.g. water availability).

Cryptogams dominant (mosses and lichens) - Swards of plants common; plants of mature physiognomy (clump sizes and forms); reproduction common at appropriate times of year or drought/rain cycle; overall health and vigour high."""@en ;
    skos:notation "4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mature phase"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growthstage-senescent
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Trees dominant	- Dominated by over-mature plants particularly in the dominant stratum; evidence of senescence in many plants, some without obvious links to disturbance. Tree crowns show signs of contracting: dead branches and decreased crown diameter and leaf area. Distorted branches and burls may be common. Dead trees may be present.

Shrubs dominant	- Dominated by old plants (thick stems and primary branches, crowns either extremely dense with much dead wood or thin and open if species sheds dead branches), particularly in the dominant stratum. Many senescent plants, some without obvious links to disturbance.

Grasses and herbs dominant	- In largely annual vegetation, reproduction is complete and plants are dying or mostly dead; in perennial vegetation, plants have lost vigour, are breaking down; large areas of soil are exposed. Litter accumulation may be high.

Cryptogams dominant (mosses and lichens) - Clear evidence of the degeneration of plants or clumps; dead older parts of plants may be conspicuous."""@en ;
    skos:notation "5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Senescent phase"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:growthstage-uneven
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Trees dominant	- Mixed size and age classes, usually identified by two or more strata dominated by the same species, but can also be sites with different species regenerating in the understorey of an older canopy.

Shrubs dominant	- Mixed size and age classes; usually two or more strata dominated by the same species, but includes sites with different species regenerating in the understorey of an older canopy.

Grasses and herbs dominant	- A mixture of mature, perennial and immature annual species present on site.

Cryptogams dominant (mosses and lichens) - A mixture of mature reproductive plants with immature regeneration."""@en ;
    skos:notation "3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Uneven age"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-0
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "<0.05 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "0" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dwarf (nw)"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-1
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "0.05-0.25 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "1" ;
    skos:prefLabel """Micro (w)
Medium (nw)"""@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition ">50.01 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Giant (w)"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-2
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "0.26-0.5 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "2" ;
    skos:prefLabel """Miniature (w)
Tall (nw)"""@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-3
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "0.51–1 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "3" ;
    skos:prefLabel """Miniature (w)
Tall (nw)"""@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-4
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "1.01–2 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "4" ;
    skos:prefLabel """Dwarf (w)
Very tall (nw)"""@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-5
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "2.01–5 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "5" ;
    skos:prefLabel """Low (w)
Extermely tall (nw)"""@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-6
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "5.01–10 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "6" ;
    skos:prefLabel """Medium (w)
Giant (nw)"""@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-7
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "10.01–20 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tall (w)"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-8
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "20.01–35 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Very tall (w)"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:height-9
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "35.01–50 m"@en ;
    skos:notation "9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Extremely tall (w)"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:indicator-growthform-fanpalm
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Forests in which fan palms (palms with branches spreading out in a fan shape, for example Licuala or Livistona) form a dense/closed stratum (80–100% crown cover) below the tallest stratum. If they form a closed stratum within the upper stratum, it would be registered as the third example in the section ‘Coding tropical/subtropical rainforests’ below"@en ;
    skos:notation "3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fan palm"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:indicator-growthform-featherpalm
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Forests in which feather palms (palms, such as coconut palms, with narrow long leaves that appear feather-like from a distance) form a dense/closed (80–100% crown cover) understorey stratum."@en ;
    skos:notation "4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Feather palm"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:indicator-growthform-fern
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Tree ferns form a dense/closed (80–100% crown cover) and discrete understorey stratum."@en ;
    skos:notation "2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fern"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:indicator-growthform-moss
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Forests in which mosses and lichens almost completely replace vascular epiphytes and vines on the trunks and in the crowns."@en ;
    skos:notation "1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moss"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:indicator-growthform-none
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "If none of the five growth forms above reaches the required level of dominance nominated, the description should record no dominant indicator growth form."@en ;
    skos:notation "6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Vine"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:indicator-growthform-vine
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Forests in which vines or twining or scrambling plants drape at least 60% of the tallest stratum and emergent trees. "@en ;
    skos:notation "5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Vine"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-macrophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: >5 macro	

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: >50% macro"""@en ;
    skos:notation "1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Macrophyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-macrophyll-mesophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: 3-5 macro and 1-4 meso

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: 30-50% macro and 10-40% meso"""@en ;
    skos:notation "2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Macrophyll-mesophyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-mesophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: >5 meso

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: >50% meso"""@en ;
    skos:notation "3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mesophyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-mesophyll-notophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: >3-5 meso and 1-4 noto

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: 30-50% meso and 10-40% noto"""@en ;
    skos:notation "4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mesophyll-notophyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-microphyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: >5 micro

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: >50% micro"""@en ;
    skos:notation "7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Microphyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-microphyll-nanophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: 3-5 micro and 1-4 nano

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: 30-50% micro and 10-40% nano"""@en ;
    skos:notation "8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Microphyll-nanophyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-nanophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: >5 nano

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: >50% nano"""@en ;
    skos:notation "9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nanophyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-notophyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: >5 noto

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: >50% noto"""@en ;
    skos:notation "5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Notophyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:leafsize-notophyll-microphyll
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Number of individual trees (maximum 10) with specified leaf sizes	: 3-5 noto and 1-4 micro

Percentage of individuals in tallest stratum with specified leaf size: 30-50% noto and 10-40% micro"""@en ;
    skos:notation "6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Notophyll-microphyll"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:life-form-nw
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "All other plants with little or no woody tissue are classified as non-woody including annuals, grasses, grass-like plants, forbs, crusts, bryophytes and algae."@en ;
    skos:notation "nw" ;
    skos:prefLabel "non-woody plants"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:maximum-height
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Maximum height"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maximum height"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:median-height
    a
        skos:Concept ,
        sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Median height"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Median height"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:montane
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Low forests dominated by Athrotaxis cupressoides and less commonly by A. selaginoides. The canopy is usually open with widely spaced trees, though dense clumps may occur. The understorey is dominated by low shrubs, grasses or mosses (Sphagnum). Shrub heights range from half to two-thirds the height of the canopy. Woody species diversity is relatively high."@en ;
    skos:notation
        "5Sw1.1 /4Mw3.0 /0Dm1.0" ,
        "M" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Montane"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:myrtle-beech
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Myrtle-beech"@en ;
    skos:narrower
        :myrtle-beech-callidendrous ,
        :myrtle-beech-implicate ,
        :myrtle-beech-thamnic ;
    skos:prefLabel "Myrtle-beech"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:myrtle-beech-callidendrous
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Medium to tall forest dominated by Nothofagus cunninghamii and/or Atherosperma moschatum. Trees well formed and widely spaced; understorey open, shady and park-like. Low diversity of woody species, which are sparse and inconspicuous in the understorey of most communities."@en ;
    skos:notation
        "6–7Dw1.1" ,
        "C" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Callidendrous"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:myrtle-beech-implicate
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Low forest, with broken uneven canopies. Dominance is usually shared by several species including Nothofagus cunninghamii, N. gunnii (rarely), Eucryphia lucida, E. milliganii, Phyllocladus aspelniifolius, Athrotaxis selaginoides, Lagerostrobus franklinii, Diselma archeri, Leptospermum nitidum, L. glaucescens, L. scoparium, L. lanigerum, Melaleuca squarrosa and Acacia mucronata. The understorey is tangled and mostly forms a continuous layer from the ground to the canopy; emergents may be present. Species diversity is relatively high for trees and shrubs."@en ;
    skos:notation
        "(E) /5Mw1.1 /4Sw3.0" ,
        "I" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Implicate"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:myrtle-beech-thamnic
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Medium height forest dominated by two to five species, mostly of: Nothofagus cunninghamii, N. gunnii (rarely), Eucryphia lucida, E. milliganii, Atherosperma moschatum, Phyllocladus aspelniifolius, Lagerostrobus franklinii and Athrotaxis selaginoides. Trees well formed; a distinct shrub layer present."@en ;
    skos:notation
        "6Mw1.1 /5Mw3.0" ,
        "T" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thamnic"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:placeholder
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "A placeholder concept used to make collections defined without members valid by having this as their sole member. This should be replace with real values"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Placeholder"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:sclerophyll-present
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "If sclerophyll species (defined above) are present in the dominant stratum, these should be recorded by adding the qualifying term ‘and sclerophylls’. If the sclerophylls can be identified, ‘sclerophyll’ should be replaced by the specific, generic or common name (for example ‘and wattles’). Where sclerophyll species are 50% of the crown cover of the canopy, this will have been recorded previously and need not be repeated."@en ;
    skos:notation "S" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sclerophyll species present"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:species-mixed
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "No one or two species combined make 50% or more of the crown cover in the tallest stratum."@en ;
    skos:notation "M" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mixed"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:species-mixed-plus-one-species
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Although no species or two species combined make up 50% of the crown cover of the dominant stratum, one species (the one species nominated) is conspicuously abundant. As above, common, generic or specific names can be used except for feather or fan palms (for example Mixed Booyong; Mixed Argyrodendron; Mixed Argyrodendron trifoliolatum or ARTRI). This floristic term can be used to nominate species of particular indicator value to the user."@en ;
    skos:notation "X" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mixed plus one species"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:species-one-or-two-species
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "The one or two species described constitute 50% or more of the crown cover of the tallest stratum. Common, generic or specific names can be used (for example Coachwood–crabapple; Ceratopetalum–Schizomeria; Ceratopetalum apetalum – Schizomeria ovata). Use species name abbreviations for coding (for example CEAPE.SCOVA) except for fan or feather palms, which should not be used as common names because they are used to denote structural features (see ‘Indicator growth forms’ below). Include sclerophyll species if they constitute 50% or more of the crown cover of the tallest stratum."@en ;
    skos:notation "S" ;
    skos:prefLabel "One or two species description"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:stratum-E
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "The tallest plants in some vegetation are so sparse that they no longer form the dominant or most significant layer. For example, a few tall Araucaria or Eucalyptus trees may rise above a closed rainforest canopy, or widely scattered eucalypts or acacias may rise above lower shrubs or grasses in semiarid regions. The tallest stratum is then classified as an ‘emergent layer’ (see the later section ‘Emergents’ on page xx for definition and discussion) and the dominant layer on which the vegetation will be classified is usually the next tallest layer."@en ;
    skos:notation "E" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Emergent"@en ;
    skos:related :emergents ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:stratum-G
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "can also be the dominant stratum, for example in places where grass cover is closed and trees are very sparse. There is no mandatory height limit on the ground layer, but it is usually less than 2.0 m tall. "@en ;
    skos:notation "G" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ground stratum"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:stratum-M
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "contains the plants with the greatest above-ground biomass. In most cases, the tallest stratum will also be the dominant stratum. Emergents are an exception (see below)."@en ;
    skos:narrower
        :stratum-M1 ,
        :stratum-M2 ;
    skos:notation "M" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mid-stratum"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:stratum-M1
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "contains the plants with the greatest above-ground biomass. In most cases, the tallest stratum will also be the dominant stratum. Emergents are an exception (see below)."@en ;
    skos:notation "M1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mid-stratum 1"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:stratum-M2
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "contains the plants with the greatest above-ground biomass. In most cases, the tallest stratum will also be the dominant stratum. Emergents are an exception (see below)."@en ;
    skos:notation "M2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mid-stratum 2"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:stratum-U
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "contains the plants with the greatest above-ground biomass. In most cases, the tallest stratum will also be the dominant stratum. Emergents are an exception (see below)."@en ;
    skos:notation "U" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dominant or upper stratum"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-growth-form-1
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Woody or herbaceous, not ephemeral"@en ;
    skos:notation "1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Emergent, permanent"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-growth-form-2
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Herbaceous, ephemeral"@en ;
    skos:notation "2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Emergent, ephemeral"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-growth-form-3
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Floating stems with leaves at the surface but roots in substrate	
Herbaceous, leaves at surface"""@en ;
    skos:notation "3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Floating stems"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-growth-form-4
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Predominantly herbaceous; for example grass mats not attached to substrate"@en ;
    skos:notation "4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Floating mats"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-growth-form-5
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition """Fully submerged with roots attached to substrate	
Herbaceous with whole plant below surface; in some cases flowers may be emergent"""@en ;
    skos:notation "5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fully submerged attached to substrate	"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-growth-form-6
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Unattached plant, submerged; for example free-floating herbs or algae"@en ;
    skos:notation "6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fully submerged, floating"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-1
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Marine waters—permanent shallow waters less than 6 m deep at low tide; includes sea bays, straits"@en ;
    skos:notation "1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marine waters"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-10
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Brackish to saline lagoons and marshes with one or more relatively narrow connections with the sea"@en ;
    skos:notation "10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lagoons and marshes"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-11
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Freshwater lagoons and marshes in the coastal zone"@en ;
    skos:notation "11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Freshwater lagoons and marshes"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-12
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Non-tidal freshwater forested wetlands"@en ;
    skos:notation "12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forested wetlands"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-13
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Permanent rivers and streams; includes waterfalls"@en ;
    skos:notation "13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Permanent rivers and streams"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-14
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Seasonal and irregular rivers and streams"@en ;
    skos:notation "14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seasonal rivers and streams"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-15
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Inland deltas (permanent)"@en ;
    skos:notation "15" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Inland deltas"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-16
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Riverine floodplains; includes seasonally flooded grassland, savanna and palm savanna; river flats; flooded river basins"@en ;
    skos:notation "16" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Riverine floodplains"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-17
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Permanent freshwater lakes (>8 ha); includes large oxbow lakes"@en ;
    skos:notation "17" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Permanent freshwater lakes"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-18
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Seasonal/intermittent freshwater lakes (>8 ha), floodplain lakes"@en ;
    skos:notation "18" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seasonal freshwater lakes"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-19
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Permanent saline/brackish lakes"@en ;
    skos:notation "19" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Permanent saline/brackish lakes"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-2
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Sub-tidal aquatic beds; includes kelp beds, seagrasses, tropical marine meadows"@en ;
    skos:notation "2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sub-tidal aquatic beds"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-20
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Seasonal/intermittent saline lakes"@en ;
    skos:notation "20" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seasonal saline lakes"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-21
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Permanent freshwater ponds (<8 ha), marshes and swamps on inorganic soils; emergent vegetation is waterlogged for at least most of the growing season"@en ;
    skos:notation "21" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Permanent freshwater ponds"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-22
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Seasonal/intermittent freshwater ponds and marshes on inorganic soils; includes billabongs, sloughs, potholes, seasonally flooded meadows, sedge marshes"@en ;
    skos:notation "22" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seasonal freshwater ponds"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-23
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Permanent saline/brackish marshes"@en ;
    skos:notation "23" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Permanent saline/brackish marshes"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-24
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Seasonal saline marshes"@en ;
    skos:notation "24" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seasonal saline marshes"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-25
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Shrub swamps; includes shrub-dominated freshwater marsh, shrub carr, alder thicket on inorganic soils"@en ;
    skos:notation "25" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shrub swamps"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-26
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Freshwater swamp forest—seasonally flooded forest or wooded swamps on inorganic soils"@en ;
    skos:notation "26" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Freshwater swamp forest"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-27
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Peatlands; includes forest, shrub or open bogs"@en ;
    skos:notation "27" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peatlands"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

:wetland-type-28
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:definition "Alpine and tundra wetlands; includes alpine meadows, tundra pools, temporary waters from snow melt"@en ;
    skos:notation "28" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Alpine and tundra wetlands"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf cs: ;
.

